            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\r\n\r\nI remember thinking about it and still not implementing. Here are the few reasons why:\r\n- It is not quite intuitive for me in the way you describe and quite complex for the way I find intuitive. Having `!abc` prompt match items not containing `abc` exactly is a two-step jump from having `abc` match items fuzzily. So I'd expect `!abc` to match items which don't match `abc` *fuzzily*. But that would require allowing extra modifies to play nicely with `!`: `!'abc` would mean \"not matches exactly\", `!^abc` - \"not matches exactly at start\", `!abc$` - not matches at the end, and `!*abc` not matches fuzzily (useful if user wants something like `!*'a`).\r\n- It adds complexity to a parsing logic. Which is already quite crowded.\r\n\r\nI'll revisit it later, maybe I'd reevaluate.",
