        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> Just in case somebody wants this now here is my implementation: [Gist](https://gist.github.com/ibutra/24a741496dd60bb2e33a045d84db42b1)\r\n> \r\n> This should be an equivalent algorithm to the one used in Mini.Pick but with added `!` operator. Note: The original sorting is preserved with the `!` operator because obviously I can't sort based on the length of the match.\r\n> \r\n> Also anybody including you echanovski feel free to use this in any way you like including inspiration ;)\r\n> \r\n> Also any feedback is of course welcome\r\n\r\nThat's quite a bottoms up implementation. I was thinking more like utilizing [`default_match()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/05f4a49cd85a67b90328a1bcbae4d9ed2a0a417b/doc/mini-pick.txt#L834) with `do_sync` argument to return the result. Like this:\r\n```lua\r\nlocal default_match = require('mini.pick').default_match\r\nlocal match_ext = function(stritems, inds, query)\r\n  if query[1] ~= '!' then return default_match(stritems, inds, query) end\r\n\r\n  -- Compute matches ignoring first '!'\r\n  local new_query = vim.list_slice(query, 2, #query)\r\n  local not_matches = default_match(stritems, inds, new_query, true)\r\n\r\n  -- Compute and return complement of the matched indexes\r\n  local res_inds_map = {}\r\n  for i = 1, #stritems do\r\n    res_inds_map[i] = true\r\n  end\r\n  for _, ind in ipairs(not_matches) do\r\n    res_inds_map[ind] = nil\r\n  end\r\n  return vim.tbl_keys(res_inds_map)\r\nend\r\n```\r\n\r\nIts only downside is that it is synchronous when query starts with '!' (should be still asynchronous when it doesn't).",
